    "knowledge_point": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine",
    "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/9)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$  \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
